WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Okay, welcome back. Welcome to video
number 21, price action. Price action is
the second weapon in our armory within the
gatekeeping process. So again, here we're
trying to cleanse our ideas. And our ideas
at this stage are still just ideas.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:52.000
And when we're monitoring price action, we
do this in conjunction with our technical
analysis. And we do this on our watch
list. And the easiest way to actually
teach you this is to show you by doing. So
what we're going to do is just actually go
straight to the computer screen. And I'm
going to show you a watch list with many
spread trades on and trades in currencies
and commodities.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:11.000
And we're going to go through this watch
list. And we're going to show you the
processes that you go through each week in
order to generate good trades. So let's go
over to the screen. And I'm going to see
you at the end of this video with a full
rundown and summary of the gatekeeping
process.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:40.980
Thank you.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Okay, so welcome back to the computer
screen.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.080
What we're going to be looking at here is
the gatekeeping process.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:01.080
And the processes that you go through for
monitoring price action and technicals.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.540
And what we're going to be looking at is a
watch list.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:10.580
So we're going to be looking at primarily
this spreadsheet.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.860
Called the watch list example.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.280
And the other spreadsheet.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.080
Which is called watch list spreads.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:28.780
Watch list spreads is basically just a
template of watch list example.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.620
And we're also going to be just going
online and getting publicly available
data.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:46.320
And we're going to be going through
processes on this watch list that you
would go through on a weekly basis when
you're starting out.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.220
So let's go through this spreadsheet.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.300
And there's quite a few concepts that you
have to pick up here.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:02.940
Firstly, when you look at this watch list,
you'll see lots of ideas on the watch
list.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:21.880
What you need to understand here is that
this is the first thing really, is that
when you're running a portfolio, you have
to look at your gross exposure that you
actually want in your portfolio.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:28.440
And then you have to look at your
portfolio, and then you have to create a
watch list to service that gross exposure.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:43.260
So when we look at this example, this
example has 24 spread trades on the watch
list within stocks.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:51.300
And we also have forex and commodities, so
energy, metals, and food.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:59.480
Now, if we're just staying within stocks,
we have 24 spread trades here.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:08.480
Now, what you're looking at when you're
seeing this spreadsheet is actually a real
example of somebody that's taken this
course.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:22.540
They've gone through the course and then
created their own watch list, and eight
weeks later, they're now running their
watch list and updating it every week.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:32.720
And when you look at it, the first thing
that you should notice is that they have
24 ideas,

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:41.820
and also they have six situations that
have actually been taken off the watch
list.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:53.640
And in fact, when you look at the
accompanying text, these spread trades
here have actually become real positions
in the portfolio.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.460
Now, what do we mean by working backwards
from your gross exposure?

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:11.620
Well, if, for example, you want to have a
portfolio of $240,000 gross exposure,
which this person actually has,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.760
then to service that portfolio, you're
going to need quite a lot of ideas.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:30.160
So, if we're working backwards from
$240,000, if this person has a
self-imposed position limit of $20,000,

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:37.200
so if they're doing a spread trade $20,000
long and $20,000 short,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:47.440
then that just means that they're going to
have 12 positions in their portfolio, or
six spread trades.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.380
So, that's going to equal $240,000 gross
exposure.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:06:04.420
So, if we're working backwards from that,
and we're going to be creating a watch
list that actually services our portfolio
well,

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:10.180
then it's pretty obvious that we can't
have just six spread trade ideas.

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:16.460
Because all that happens is, when you're
running your real portfolio,

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:23.480
we're admitting to ourselves that clearly
we're not superheroes.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.060
We're going to get trades wrong.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:30.720
So, at any one point in time, you're going
to have trades that are winning and trades
that are losing.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:40.400
And if you don't have a well-maintained,
updated, and fresh portfolio,

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:46.200
as in lots of new ideas coming onto the
watch list,

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:55.780
then that watch list can't service your
portfolio with good trades to replace
losing trades.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:03.460
So, what you're trying to do here when
you're running your watch list is two
things.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.900
Firstly, you're trying to stop yourself
making bad trades.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:15.900
So, your ideas go on your watch list and
you're stopping yourself from firstly
doing bad trades

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:21.500
and also entering potentially good trades
at the wrong time.

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:23.800
That's the first thing.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:28.160
We'll come back to that a little bit later
when we look at a specific example.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:36.880
But secondly, what you're trying to do is
you're trying to spoil yourself with good
ideas and good trades.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:46.180
So, if we just look at the numbers for a
second, we've got 24 trades, or 24 ideas,
sorry,

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.300
at this stage on the watch list.

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:53.120
And six have actually been taken off.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:56.800
And they've become real positions.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:03.460
So, that's going to leave 18 ideas on the
watch list.

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:14.520
Which means, if they've got, if this
trader has six spread trades, so 12
positions,

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.100
they have six spread trades in their
portfolio,

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:26.240
it means they've got three times as many
ideas on their watch list as they do live
positions

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:27.500
in their real portfolio.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:37.280
And as a general rule of thumb, what you
should really be doing is your watch list
should have

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:46.800
a minimum, really, of two and a half times
the amount of ideas as versus real trades
or real

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:48.140
positions in your portfolio.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:52.000
So, you should use that as a ratio.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:59.660
So, whatever the size of your portfolio in
terms of positions, your watch list should
really have a minimum of two and a half
times

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:03.400
that in the amount of ideas that you have
on your watch list.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:06.800
So, that's the first thing.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:11.180
Now, let's go through the spreadsheets a
little bit and start breaking things down.

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:15.920
And we'll come on to some other concepts
when we look at a live example.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:26.980
So, the example that we're going to look
at here is EasyJet, IAG, this idea that's
on the watch list here.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:34.080
And EasyJet and IAG, they both trade in
the United Kingdom on the London Stock
Exchange.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.280
And EasyJet is a low-cost airline carrier.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:46.540
And IAG is a global airline carrier,
primarily Europe and Transatlantic, but
it's a global carrier.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.980
And IAG is basically British Airways and
Iberia.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:10:01.440
And IAG, International Consolidated
Airlines Group, is the holding company for
those two brands, for those two companies.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:10.960
So, we're looking at the idea here on the
watch list of long EasyJet, short IAG.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:21.320
And the long entry and the short entry of
this idea gives a spread of 438 on the
watch list.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:25.340
And we haven't actually entered this
position.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:28.560
What this is, is a reference price.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:38.440
So, we're referencing the time, the price
of these stocks at the time that we put
them on the watch list.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:43.000
And this gives us a reference spread of
438.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:52.280
So, when we scroll across from here, we
have this repeated again here.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:55.740
But we also have the current situation.

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:02.120
And the current situation, this is what
you update every week.

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:07.600
So, the current situation gives EasyJet at
10.31.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:16.260
And IAG, International Consolidated
Airlines Group, at 258 spot 9.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:25.100
And when we scroll across here, this gives
us a spread of 398.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:35.660
Which means, overall, this spread from the
reference price, when it originally went
on the watch list, is down 9%.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:38.420
And it's been on the watch list for 7
weeks.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:44.140
And this is the second concept that you
have to pick up here.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:54.340
So, concept number one, the amount of
trades that you need, or the amount of
ideas that you need on your watch list to
service your portfolio backed out.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:12:01.860
So, spoiling yourself with a good amount
of ideas that become good trades.

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:03.980
So, don't forget that concept.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:15.520
The concept that we're moving on to now is
really the primary reason why we have a
gatekeeping process and why we run a watch
list.

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:24.040
And that's really because we want to make
sure that we don't put on bad trades with
our real money.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:29.960
But also, we want to make sure that we're
not mistiming good trades.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:42.140
So, we're back to this example, again, of
buying an asset at, say, $100 or a stock
at $100.

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:47.540
And then, at this stage, it's an idea.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:56.800
And what you have to assume is that
everybody in the world thinks when they
have an idea for a trade, that it's a
fantastic idea.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.900
Otherwise, you wouldn't consider
committing capital to it.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:05.240
So, just assume that every idea you get,
you're obviously going to think that it's
a great idea.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:07.720
You know, we're all only human.

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:09.440
People think that all the time.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:10.260
Of course, they do.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.740
But that's the typical retail trader
mistake.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:21.640
The typical retail trader mistake is to
get an idea and then just put the trade on
straight away.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:34.040
Well, the professional trader realises
that they're only human and they have a
gatekeeping process to make sure that they
don't do anything stupid.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:46.300
So, imagine if you buy, you think you have
this great idea and you buy this stock at
$100 and you think that a good stop loss
is $90.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:50.140
And then, you just put the trade on
straight away at $100.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:52.280
Well, at that stage, it's just an idea.

00:13:53.420 --> 00:14:01.080
So, you put the trade on at $100 and then
you get stopped out at $90 and then it
goes to $500.

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:05.060
Well, that's the cardinal sin in trading.

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:14.700
What you've actually just done is you've
put on an idea and an idea at that stage
is just an idea.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:16.320
It's not a trade.

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:25.420
And you put this idea on straight away,
you get stopped out, so you lose money,
but then you'll prove to be right.

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:43.940
What you could have done was just put the
trade on the watch list and then waited
for that idea to actually start working
before you committed capital.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:55.940
Instead, you committed capital straight
away and you just got stopped out and then
you missed the opportunity to make $400 on
the upside.

00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:01.900
Now, think of it in the opposite
situation.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:07.140
You've got this idea and you put it on
straight away at $100.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.740
You get stopped out at $90 and then it
goes to $0.

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:29.480
Well, that's just proven to be a bad idea
and a bad trade, but you've just lost 10%
and you could have avoided losing that 10%
by just sticking it on a watch list and
monitoring the price action and never
committing capital to a trade that wasn't
working.

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:31.440
So, you would have saved yourself 10%.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:44.780
This is why we have a gatekeeping process
to stop ourselves mistiming potentially
good trades and to stop ourselves getting
into bad trades full stop.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:46.200
Period.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:16:05.900
So, you have to have this gatekeeping
process and make sure that you go through
it each week and spoil yourself with
enough ideas that they become, or a good
portion of them become, good trades.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:19.720
And don't forget, everything that ends up
on your watch list is an idea that ticks
all the boxes in terms of agreeing with
your fundamental views of the world, the
industry view and the stock view.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:26.740
So, by the time it ends up on the watch
list, it's in full fundamental agreement
with your views.

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:39.140
And then, what you're trying to do is
cleanse your idea and you're trying to add
value to your idea by putting it on a
watch list and monitoring the price action
and technicals.

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:46.460
So, you're trying to add value to the idea
and get into the idea when the price
action is good and the technicals are
good.

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:51.180
Now, this example here, EasyJet IAG.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:58.780
This has lost 9% on the watch list over
seven weeks.

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:04.040
So, imagine if this trader just put this
idea on straight away, they'd be losing
9%.

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:09.720
That's not to say this trade isn't a good
trade.

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:11.740
It could actually be a phenomenal trade.

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:22.180
But what we're trying to do here is we're
trying to make sure that we get in at a
good price and we're trying to make sure
that we just don't enter a trade that's
bad full stop.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:26.480
So, let's have a look at the breakdown of
these seven weeks.

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:34.280
And what we can see by scrolling across is
that the price action is pretty choppy.

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:39.360
And week six, this is where pretty much
all of the underperformance has come from.

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:46.860
And when we look at the price action in
absolute terms, it's not working.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:48.180
This trade is not working.

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:51.580
So, we shouldn't be committing capital to
this trade.

00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:56.620
Now, when we look at all the rest of our
trades, what are we looking at?

00:17:56.740 --> 00:18:02.000
Well, we're looking at a bunch of ideas
that are working and a bunch of ideas that
are not working.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:06.500
Well, the two back-of-the-envelope rules,
if you like.

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:09.400
You know, there are no rules in terms of
committing capital.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:12.560
That's the third concept we're going to
come on to in a minute.

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:33.900
So, the two kind of back-of-the-envelope
rules of thumb when committing capital and
considering price action is that, firstly,
you shouldn't commit capital to losing
trades or losing ideas.

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:36.060
That's the first thing.

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:44.060
Secondly, what you're looking for is
consistency in price action, so
consistently positive returns.

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:53.300
And if we look further down here, we can
see potential trades, so ideas.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:19:03.940
We've got ideas here that could be
potential trades where the price action is
positive and the weekly return.

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:06.360
Looks fairly consistent.

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:13.440
So, this one, SWFT, long and short, cat.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:22.200
And also, HTZ and cat.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.380
Positive and fairly consistent price
action.

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:32.540
So, we're not going to go into every
single idea on this watch list.

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:37.840
You can do that in your own time because
you've got the spreadsheet in the
downloads area.

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:41.660
But, just know the concepts here.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:50.460
So, you understand that you don't commit
capital to positions or ideas that are
losing.

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:59.480
And you don't commit capital to losing
trades or losing ideas that have choppy
price action.

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:02.740
What you're looking for is the opposite.

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:05.800
You're looking for positive price action.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:09.360
And you're looking for consistency in
returns.

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:21.840
If you do that over your lifetime, this
will not only save you a lot of money in
the gatekeeping process, but it will also
make you quite a lot of money as well.

00:20:23.300 --> 00:20:27.440
Because you're cleansing and adding value
to every idea that you've got.

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:29.880
And that can only be a good thing.

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:34.640
So, what we're going to do now, now we've
picked up on those three concepts.

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:36.600
So, we'll just run through those again.

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:42.420
Enough ideas that turn into good trades.

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:46.220
So, a proportion of those trades will turn
into good trades.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:49.840
Enough ideas to service your portfolio.

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:52.840
That's the first thing.

00:20:54.300 --> 00:21:00.540
Secondly, most important one, the reasons
why we have a gatekeeping process.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:10.620
To stop ourselves mistiming potentially
good ideas and to stop ourselves putting
on bad trades, full stop, period.

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:16.100
Three, committing capital.

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:18.700
And again, there's no rule book here.

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:20.900
We'll come back to this in a second.

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:26.820
There's no rule book for committing
capital, but a back of the envelope rule
of thumb.

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:36.160
Don't commit capital to trades or ideas
that are not working on your watch list
and have choppy price action.

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:43.620
You want the opposite, positive price
action with consistency or showing
consistency in returns.

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:47.940
So, they're the three concepts that you
need to get used to straight away.

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:56.120
Now, the process we're going to go through
now is I'm going to simulate a process of
updating your watch list.

00:21:58.120 --> 00:22:15.740
Now, what you'll notice is all of these
trades here, all of these ideas, are
actually within, if we go to the bottom of
the sheet, the spreadsheet, are actually
within their own individual sheets.

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:21.940
So, what we're going to look at here is
EasyJet IAG again, as an example.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:25.060
So, we're going to click in to this sheet.

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:36.500
And we can see that EasyJet International
Consolidated Airlines Group has its own
sheet.

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:42.260
And within that sheet, we've got the data
