through is the process that you go through
in terms of updating your watch list.

00:33:39.640 --> 00:33:47.660
So, updating the last five days, updating
your top sheet and inferring the price
action.

00:33:48.440 --> 00:33:51.820
And now we're moving on to how you would
actually go and commit capital.

00:33:52.420 --> 00:33:54.920
Also, we're going to change this cell to
week 8.

00:34:00.240 --> 00:34:02.020
So, don't forget those processes.

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:06.200
You have to do this for every spread
trade.

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:07.880
You know, you have to do this as well.

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:14.620
For example, for Hewlett-Packard, Cisco,
if that was one of your ideas, you just do
the same thing.

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:16.580
Select five rows.

00:34:18.180 --> 00:34:18.620
Insert.

00:34:19.280 --> 00:34:20.000
Get the data.

00:34:20.600 --> 00:34:21.520
Update the charts.

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:23.040
Right mouse, select data.

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:24.380
Go through the processes.

00:34:25.280 --> 00:34:28.280
This is going to save you and make you a
lot of money over your lifetime.

00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:30.020
It's well worth doing.

00:34:31.160 --> 00:34:35.020
And once you're used to it, it only takes
you a few hours every week.

00:34:36.080 --> 00:34:39.400
You know, I tend to do this on a Saturday
morning for a couple of hours.

00:34:40.720 --> 00:34:50.180
And what you do is, when you've updated
these processes, you know, you've got a
bunch of ideas here.

00:34:50.560 --> 00:35:00.380
And if you have enough high-quality ideas
on your watch list, all that's going to
happen is that your watch list is going to
start servicing your portfolio.

00:35:02.020 --> 00:35:05.520
Because good trades are going to present
themselves to you.

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:09.240
So good ideas are going to turn into good
trades.

00:35:10.180 --> 00:35:12.440
And they're going to end up in your
portfolio.

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:18.020
So what you're going to see week to week
is trades will present themselves to you.

00:35:18.540 --> 00:35:22.000
They'll pop their head up and wave their
hand and say,

00:35:22.480 --> 00:35:25.720
Hello, Mr. Trader, I'm now working.

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:28.740
I'm one of your ideas and I'm now working.

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:33.000
And that's usually a positive sign to
start committing capital.

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:35.900
So make sure you've got enough ideas.

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:40.080
Make sure you remember the concepts that
we went through at the beginning of the
video.

00:35:40.700 --> 00:35:43.200
And go through these processes every week.

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:46.100
Now, moving on to capital commitment.

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:50.160
And this might be a little bit difficult
to get your head around.

00:35:50.160 --> 00:35:56.640
But, unfortunately, you cannot go down to
the local bookstore.

00:35:57.040 --> 00:36:03.960
You cannot go on Amazon.com and buy a book
called, In All Situations, This Is How You
Deploy Capital.

00:36:04.960 --> 00:36:07.020
Because it just doesn't exist.

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:09.840
Every retail trader looks for a rule book.

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:12.200
Every professional trader knows there
isn't one.

00:36:12.840 --> 00:36:19.520
Because in terms of committing capital,
you have to take every trade on its own
individual merits.

00:36:20.240 --> 00:36:25.500
So let's take this EasyJet IAG example on
its individual merits.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:32.180
And we already know at this stage that
from the reference prices, when we put it
on the watch list,

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:36.840
that the price action is not positive to
this point.

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:47.260
And the technicals, when we look at them,
are not exactly great.

00:36:48.260 --> 00:36:55.120
So, we're kind of here nor there.

00:36:55.840 --> 00:36:59.520
When we look at the technicals, we're in
what we'd call an amber situation.

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:03.140
We're kind of range trading here.

00:37:04.900 --> 00:37:06.640
So, this is the last two years.

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:13.340
What we can do is take a line, just to
illustrate this a little bit better.

00:37:17.180 --> 00:37:28.020
So, let's just make this red and increase
its thickness a little bit.

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:32.200
Give us a better representation of what
this is actually doing.

00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:39.700
So, we've got negative price action
overall, since it was referenced onto the
watch list.

00:37:40.660 --> 00:37:44.720
And we've got choppy price action, so
overall price action is not great.

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:51.260
We're now looking at the technicals, and
the technicals really are giving us an
amber situation.

00:37:51.620 --> 00:37:53.620
It doesn't take long to deduce these
things.

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:07.100
So, we're kind of in this range here.

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:13.600
And when we look at the overall
technicals, you know, they look good.

00:38:14.100 --> 00:38:25.280
So, we've got a two-year chart here, where
over the last two years, we've got a
really strong positive trend, and now
we're trending sideways in a range.

00:38:26.360 --> 00:38:29.280
And overall, we've got a very good
positive trend as well.

00:38:29.900 --> 00:38:31.540
So, the overall picture looks good here.

00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:36.600
And what we're trying to do here is work
out when we should commit capital at the
right time.

00:38:37.700 --> 00:38:39.680
Well, we've got this nice support level
here.

00:38:41.780 --> 00:38:43.160
We've got this resistance level.

00:38:44.360 --> 00:38:45.420
And we're kind of here nor there.

00:38:45.700 --> 00:38:47.680
We look like we're in a short-term range.

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:52.240
If we look at the last two years, you
know, this is a big move.

00:38:53.080 --> 00:38:56.840
You know, do we really want to be the fool
that buys this spread right at the top?

00:38:57.680 --> 00:38:59.560
You know, we want to see positive price
action here.

00:38:59.620 --> 00:39:03.540
We want to be proved correct that this is
going to continue to go higher.

00:39:04.420 --> 00:39:11.220
So, what I'm going to do first, a couple
of logical things, is work out what this
resistance level is here.

00:39:13.880 --> 00:39:15.480
So, let's have a look.

00:39:15.480 --> 00:39:20.640
This is at some point in February, so
towards the beginning of 2013.

00:39:22.040 --> 00:39:31.400
And if we go through the data on the
left-hand side, we can see, actually, that
this 453.84

00:39:31.400 --> 00:39:36.960
seems to be the trading high of the
spread.

00:39:40.580 --> 00:39:44.140
So, that's our resistance level, 453.84.

00:39:46.580 --> 00:39:51.660
And now, if you were feeling brave, what
you could do here, and this is always an
option.

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:54.200
So, the first option is non-participation.

00:39:54.420 --> 00:39:55.340
That's always an option.

00:39:56.060 --> 00:40:03.780
The second option is to commit all your
capital, which in this situation, in an
amber technical

00:40:03.780 --> 00:40:10.360
situation with not positive price action,
is probably a bit stupid.

00:40:13.240 --> 00:40:18.140
You might as well not have the watch list
at all, or the gatekeeping process at all,
if you commit

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:19.000
all your capital here.

00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:24.080
But option number three is to commit some
capital.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:31.580
So, I would say, if you were feeling
brave, you could actually go in and maybe
take what

00:40:31.580 --> 00:40:33.320
we call a starter position.

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:35.540
So, you start with something.

00:40:35.540 --> 00:40:42.820
So, let's say, for example, you thought
your full position size was going to be
longer

00:40:42.820 --> 00:40:47.780
$10,000 of EasyJet, short $10,000 of IAG.

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:57.840
Well, in this situation, if you're feeling
brave, what you could do is go and deploy
$5,000 on

00:40:57.840 --> 00:41:00.160
each side, so you put half a position on.

00:41:01.220 --> 00:41:05.020
And if you put half a position on in these
situations, it gives you choices.

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:06.460
It gives you some choices.

00:41:07.160 --> 00:41:11.200
So, let's say, for example, we know this
is at 427.

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:14.780
Let's say we put this spread on at 427.

00:41:15.460 --> 00:41:17.780
Well, what would a 10% stop loss be?

00:41:20.420 --> 00:41:21.600
So, let's work this out.

00:41:21.600 --> 00:41:23.780
We just multiply that by 0.9.

00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:29.440
So, 427 times 0.9 is 384.

00:41:30.820 --> 00:41:35.600
So, let's change the colour of this line
to green.

00:41:36.800 --> 00:41:39.320
And we'll call this our theoretical stop
loss.

00:41:40.080 --> 00:41:42.720
So, this would be at 384.

00:41:45.240 --> 00:41:46.580
So, around here.

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:51.500
So, we've got this nice support level at
400 or 4.

00:41:52.300 --> 00:41:55.740
We've got a theoretical stop loss at 384.

00:41:56.260 --> 00:41:59.420
But that stop loss is on a full position.

00:42:02.080 --> 00:42:09.160
So, a 10% stop loss on a full position is
fairly sensible.

00:42:09.800 --> 00:42:14.240
It's wide enough to give us a chance of
making money over 1 to 3 months.

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:17.560
You'd obviously go and double-check that
with the ATR.

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:21.080
And we'll do that in subsequent videos.

00:42:23.100 --> 00:42:26.340
But this is on a full position.

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:28.940
So, what would happen if you had a starter
position?

00:42:29.600 --> 00:42:33.860
Well, the first choice it would give you
is that you could actually widen your stop
loss.

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:40.720
So, if you put on half a position, you can
double the stop loss.

00:42:42.420 --> 00:42:52.860
So, we can take 427 and multiply that by
0.8 to give it a 20% stop loss, which
gives you a stop loss of 341.

00:42:54.060 --> 00:42:54.500
Here.

00:42:56.980 --> 00:42:58.280
And that gives you choices.

00:42:59.120 --> 00:43:08.160
You know, you could also, for example, if
this fell off a cliff in the future and
got to this level,

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:13.880
you know, you could actually, if it
started showing positive price action
again,

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:20.560
go and purchase the other 50% and end up
with your stop loss 10% below your average
price.

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:26.220
So, that gives us our first choice.

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:32.500
The second choice it also gives us is that
if it continues showing positive price
action

00:43:32.500 --> 00:43:37.340
and goes through this resistance level of
453.84,

00:43:38.220 --> 00:43:43.680
then we could actually go and buy the
other half of the position

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:47.760
if it's showing and agreeing with our view
and breaking to new highs.

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:54.580
And you would end up with an average price
below the resistance level,

00:43:54.580 --> 00:43:57.360
which could become the new support.

00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:05.660
And your stop loss would be well below
your average price if you put a 10% stop
loss on it.

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:09.120
So, a starter position does give you
choices.

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:13.160
But, as I said, that's, you know, if
you're feeling brave.

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:17.100
And there's, again, no rules here to
committing capital.

00:44:17.480 --> 00:44:22.020
Unfortunately, you can't buy that book off
Amazon.com.

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:25.840
How to commit capital to all trading
situations.

00:44:27.400 --> 00:44:28.380
There are no rules.

00:44:28.820 --> 00:44:30.280
You have to just use your common sense.

00:44:31.160 --> 00:44:32.680
You know, the best thing you can do as a
trader

00:44:32.680 --> 00:44:38.500
is to generate as many high-quality ideas
as you can

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:41.660
and then have a gatekeeping process

00:44:41.660 --> 00:44:45.820
and try to time ideas.

00:44:46.580 --> 00:44:49.240
So, good ideas that end up becoming good
trades

00:44:49.240 --> 00:44:53.520
and try to not commit capital to bad
trades.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:56.120
So, you have that gatekeeping process in
